    <fr:title text="List">List</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex> be a natural number. A <html:strong>list</html:strong> of length <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex> is an ordered collection of <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex> elements.
    <fr:tex display="block">
        (x_1, x_2, \dots , x_n)
    </fr:tex>
    Two lists are equal if and only if they have the same length and the same elements in the same order.
</html:p>
    <html:strong>Addition in Lists</html:strong>
    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex> be a natural number. Let <fr:tex display="inline">(x_1, x_2, \dots , x_n)</fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline">(y_1, y_2, \dots , y_n)</fr:tex> be lists of length <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex>. The <html:strong>sum</html:strong> of these lists is the list <fr:tex display="inline">(x_1 + y_1, x_2 + y_2, \dots , x_n + y_n)</fr:tex>.
</html:p>
    <html:strong>Additive Inverse in Lists</html:strong>
    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex> be a natural number. Let <fr:tex display="inline">x=(x_1, x_2, \dots , x_n)</fr:tex> be a list of length <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex>. The <html:strong>additive inverse</html:strong> of this list is the list <fr:tex display="inline">-x=(-x_1, -x_2, \dots , -x_n)</fr:tex>.
</html:p>
    <html:strong>Scalar Multiplication in Lists</html:strong>
    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex> be a natural number. Let <fr:tex display="inline">x=(x_1, x_2, \dots , x_n)</fr:tex> be a list of length <fr:tex display="inline">n</fr:tex>. Let <fr:tex display="inline">c</fr:tex> be a real number. The <html:strong>scalar multiplication</html:strong> of <fr:tex display="inline">c</fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline">x</fr:tex> is the list <fr:tex display="inline">cx=(cx_1, cx_2, \dots , cx_n)</fr:tex>.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
